    "score": 28.243089646713713,
    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Argentina helicopter crash: the sports stars who died\nauthor: Caroline Davies\ndate: 2015-03-10\n---\nChampion sailor Florence Arthaud, Olympic gold medalist swimmer Camille Muffat and Olympic boxer Alexis Vastine were among 1o people killed when two helicopters filming a French reality TV series crashed into each other in Argentina.\n\nCamille Muffat, 25\n\nThe Olympic swimmer took three medals at the 2012 London Games: gold in the 400-metre freestyle, silver in the 200-metre freestyle and bronze in the 200-metre freestyle relay. She surprised the swimming world by announcing her retirement last July amid rumours of a disagreement with her coach, ending a career that had spanned more than a decade at elite international level.\n\nBorn in Nice, Muffat was made a Knight (Chevalier) of the L\u00e9gion d'Honneur in 2013. She had intended to compete in the 2016 Rio Games, but said on announcing her retirement: \"I knew becoming an Olympic champion in 2016 would be 100 times more complicated.\" However, she had not ruled out a comeback.\n\n\"Camille was a very determined girl who could make very strong choices and face up to them. She had dedicated a lot of her life to swimming to become Olympic champion, and her objective since her retirement was to make a success of her [personal] life,\" her friend and agent Sophie Kamoun told the Associated Press.\n\n\"She had a lot of projects that made her happy, and this [reality TV] show was one of them. I spoke to her on the phone two days ago and she told me she'd spent a fabulous week, one of the best of her life.\"\n\nAlexis Vastine, 28\n\nThe Olympic boxer was born in Pont-Audemer in France. He won a bronze medal at the Beijing Games in 2008 in the light welterweight category. At the 2012 London Games, he was eliminated in the quarter-finals in a decision that was seen by many as unjust.\n\n\"It feels like I've lost a member of my family. He was my friend, he was like my little brother,\" Dominique Nato, former technical director of the French Boxing Federation, told AP. \"I had a lot of respect for him. No one will forget him.\"\n\nFlorence Arthaud, 57\n\nThe yachtswoman was acclaimed as a pioneer for women"
